Threat-Informed Design Thinking
The authentication design addresses the following risks:
- anonymous access to repair and tenant-related data
- fake or unauthenticated repair submissions
- inability to trace user activity
- exposure of internal repair workflows

Using Django’s built-in authentication reduces the risk of insecure password handling and unreliable session management.

Consequences

Positive
- improves security
- supports realistic user workflows
- enables permission-based testing
- follows Django conventions
- avoids custom password/security implementation
- supports future role-based features

Negative
- adds more templates and views
- users must log in before accessing protected pages
- requires permission testing to confirm correct access behaviour

Testing Strategy
This decision should be tested by verifying:
- users can register
- users can log in
- invalid login details are rejected
- users are redirected after login
- users are redirected after logout
- anonymous users cannot access protected pages

Final Outcome
Authentication changed the application from an open prototype into a more secure system with identifiable users and controlled access. This supports Assessment 4 requirements and provides a foundation for permission boundaries, service logic, exception handling, and testing.
